<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kasivisvesvara Temple, Lakkundi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kasivisvesvara Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakkundi">Lakkundi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadag">Gadag</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, built in the late Western Chalukya period, probably in the twelfth century. Dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, it is celebrated for the exceptionally fine and dense carving of its doorways and outer walls and is considered one of the finest examples of later Chalukya architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kasivisvesvara-temple-lakkundi.jpg" alt="Kasivisvesvara Temple, Lakkundi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P. Madhusudan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:11th_century_Kasivisvesvara_temple,_Lakkundi,_Karnataka_India_-_12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakkundi">Lakkundi</a>, about 12 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadag">Gadag</a>, is a shortened form of Lokkigundi, a name found in inscriptions in the village and far beyond it. The earliest, dated 790 CE, was found on a stone slab that washermen were using at a step well. The town grew rich after 973 CE, when Taila II revolted against the Rashtrakuta king Karkka II and founded the line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Later Chalukyas</a>, and it flourished under his son Satyasraya Irivabedanga. It became a major city with a mint and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Jain temples, and in 1192 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king Ballala II made it his capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple, also called the Kavatalesvara or Kashi Vishvanatha temple, stands at the south of the village. It has two shrines on one axis, facing each other: the larger, facing east, is dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and holds a linga three feet high; the smaller was for the sun god Surya, whose image has long been lost. A gudha-mandapa and a ranga-mandapa join them, on a moulded platform with steps. The towers over the shrines are damaged, and the roof of the mandapa has gone.</p><p>Its builders combined the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Nagara</a>, the southern Dravida and the Vesara styles; the pilasters carry miniature northern towers, and the outer walls show scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> and the Shiva Purana. The doorways are bands of carving with jewels, people, amorous couples, creepers and lotuses, and over the Surya shrine the god rides a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> of seven horses driven by Aruna. Many figures of deities in the niches were mutilated, apparently on purpose.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Scholars see the temple as the high point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakkundi">Lakkundi</a> school, whose craftsmen worked in soapstone where the rival Sudi school kept to sandstone; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> artisans of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> took up the Lakkundi approach. Henry Cousens compared the door carving to perforated filigree and called it one of the most ornate temples of the region where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> is spoken. The architectural historian Dhaky called it a landmark in the medieval architecture of Karnatadesa, and Adam Hardy the richest and most extraordinary of the temples at Lakkundi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kasivisvesvara Temple, Lakkundi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Chalukyas developed a richly ornamented style in soapstone and fine-grained stone, with stepped plans, lathe-turned pillars and dense surface carving, seen in the Mahadeva temple at Itagi, praised in its inscription as an emperor among temples, the <b>Kasivishveshvara</b> temple at Lakkundi and the temples of Gadag and Dambal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
